1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,468
Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,468 --> 00:00:15,929
Tonight we are going to drift, very slowly, through a city built on water.

3
00:00:15,929 --> 00:00:22,423
You do not need to follow every detail, and you do not need to stay awake until the end.

4
00:00:22,423 --> 00:00:26,556
Think of this as a long, unhurried ride in a small boat.

5
00:00:26,556 --> 00:00:34,157
The water will hold you up, and if you fall asleep along the way, the boat will simply keep gliding on.

6
00:00:35,657 --> 00:00:39,254
Let your body settle wherever you are lying.

7
00:00:39,254 --> 00:00:43,014
Let your jaw loosen, and your forehead soften.

8
00:00:43,014 --> 00:00:50,126
Take one slow breath in, and let it out as gently as an oar slips back into calm water.

9
00:00:50,126 --> 00:00:53,641
Then take another, and let that one go too.

10
00:00:55,141 --> 00:01:01,175
The year is somewhere in the later fifteen hundreds, in the Republic of Venice.

11
00:01:01,175 --> 00:01:03,695
It is an evening in early autumn.

12
00:01:03,695 --> 00:01:15,839
The warm days are shortening, the air over the lagoon is mild and damp, and the bells of the city's churches have just finished ringing for the end of the day.

13
00:01:15,839 --> 00:01:21,338
Lamps are being lit in windows, one after another, all along the canals.

14
00:01:22,838 --> 00:01:29,251
For the rest of tonight, we will float from one quiet corner of the city to the next.

15
00:01:29,251 --> 00:01:43,434
We will sit in a gondola, rest beside an old well, cross a crowded wooden bridge that is slowly emptying, and look out across the lagoon at distant fires on a little island of glassmakers.

16
00:01:43,434 --> 00:01:48,715
And somewhere along the way, I hope, the water will rock you to sleep.

17
00:01:58,216 --> 00:02:09,903
Venice sits in the middle of a wide, shallow lagoon, a great sheet of salt water sheltered from the open Adriatic by long, low strips of sand.

18
00:02:09,903 --> 00:02:14,841
Out here, the water is rarely deeper than a person standing.

19
00:02:14,841 --> 00:02:23,154
Marsh grass grows on the mudbanks, and at low tide the flats shine like pewter under the evening sky.

20
00:02:24,654 --> 00:02:37,162
The city itself is made of more than a hundred small islands, crowded so closely together that the narrow channels between them have become streets of water.

21
00:02:37,162 --> 00:02:48,953
Bridges join one island to the next, hundreds of them, most of them small humped arches of brick and stone, just wide enough for two people to pass.

22
00:02:50,453 --> 00:02:56,931
Beneath every church and palace and ordinary house, hidden in the mud, there is a forest.

23
00:02:56,931 --> 00:03:09,523
Builders drove countless wooden piles, mostly oak and larch, deep into the soft ground, packed them tightly together, and laid platforms of timber and stone across the tops.

24
00:03:09,523 --> 00:03:14,764
Sealed away from the air, in the wet clay, the wood keeps for centuries.

25
00:03:14,764 --> 00:03:18,549
The whole city rests quietly on those buried trunks.

26
00:03:20,049 --> 00:03:31,839
In this evening of the later fifteen hundreds, perhaps a hundred and seventy thousand people live here, which makes Venice one of the largest cities in Europe.

27
00:03:31,839 --> 00:03:47,782
Merchants, sailors, glassmakers, weavers, boatbuilders, priests, servants, and a great many children, all packed onto these little islands, with the tide rising and falling gently around their doorsteps twice a day.

28
00:03:49,282 --> 00:03:54,419
There are no carts here, and no horses clattering through the lanes.

29
00:03:54,419 --> 00:03:57,517
Everything that is heavy travels by boat.

30
00:03:57,517 --> 00:04:10,209
Barrels of wine, sacks of flour, firewood, bricks, bales of wool, baskets of fish, all of it floats into the city on flat-bottomed barges, pushed along with long poles.

31
00:04:10,209 --> 00:04:15,119
So the sound of Venice in the evening is not the sound of wheels.

32
00:04:15,119 --> 00:04:20,408
It is the sound of water, and footsteps, and voices echoing off stone.

33
00:04:21,908 --> 00:04:24,028
Tonight the lagoon is calm.

34
00:04:24,028 --> 00:04:31,175
A faint mist lies over the water, and the low line of the city seems to float on top of it.

35
00:04:31,175 --> 00:04:46,569
The bell towers rise out of the haze, one here, one there, and the light from a few hundred windows quivers on the surface below, broken into little golden pieces by the slow movement of the tide.

36
00:04:48,069 --> 00:04:55,285
Near the edge of a mudbank, a fisherman in a small flat boat is drawing in his last net of the day.

37
00:04:55,285 --> 00:05:06,146
He works slowly, hand over hand, letting the water drain through the mesh, and lifting out a few silvery fish, which he lays in a basket at his feet.

38
00:05:06,146 --> 00:05:16,570
Then he coils the net neatly in the bottom of the boat, picks up his pole, and pushes off towards home, leaving a long, slow ripple behind him.

39
00:05:18,070 --> 00:05:24,390
Out on the water, rows of wooden posts mark the safe channels through the shallows.

40
00:05:24,390 --> 00:05:31,623
They lean a little, in pairs and threes, tied together at the top, dark against the pale water.

41
00:05:31,623 --> 00:05:38,475
A heron stands beside one of them, perfectly still, on one long leg, watching the surface.

42
00:05:38,475 --> 00:05:44,033
It has been standing there for a long time, and it is in no hurry at all.

43
00:05:45,533 --> 00:05:53,877
Beyond the city, to the east, a long, thin island of sand and pine trees lies between the lagoon and the open sea.

44
00:05:53,877 --> 00:06:08,077
On a still night like this one, if you listen very carefully, you can just hear the sea on the far side of it, a slow hush, and then another, like someone breathing in a deep and peaceful sleep.

45
00:06:08,077 --> 00:06:11,517
The lagoon itself makes almost no sound at all.

46
00:06:21,017 --> 00:06:26,583
At the foot of a small stone landing, a gondola is waiting for you.

47
00:06:26,583 --> 00:06:35,388
It is long and narrow, about as long as five people lying head to foot, and it sits very low in the water.

48
00:06:35,388 --> 00:06:42,948
It is painted entirely black, and it gleams softly, like a polished shoe, in the lamplight.

49
00:06:44,447 --> 00:06:56,360
Not so long ago, the gondolas of Venice were painted in every colour, with gilded carvings and bright cloth and silk cushions, each family trying to outshine the next.

50
00:06:56,360 --> 00:07:06,419
In fifteen sixty two, the Republic passed a law against such displays of wealth on the water, and from then on, the gondolas were made black.

51
00:07:06,419 --> 00:07:08,059
Some people complained.

52
00:07:08,059 --> 00:07:16,192
But on an evening like this, the plain dark boats look calm and dignified, gliding along like long, quiet shadows.

53
00:07:17,692 --> 00:07:22,840
Look closely, and you will see that the gondola is not quite straight.

54
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:31,592
One side is built a little fuller than the other, so that the boat leans and balances against the push of a single oar.

55
00:07:31,592 --> 00:07:37,550
At the front, a tall, flat blade of iron rises from the prow, catching the light.

56
00:07:37,550 --> 00:07:46,449
In the middle, there is a small cabin, a little wooden frame covered in black cloth, with tiny windows and a low doorway.

57
00:07:47,949 --> 00:07:50,504
Step down carefully into the boat.

58
00:07:50,504 --> 00:07:54,263
It dips, and sways, and then steadies beneath you.

59
00:07:54,263 --> 00:07:59,750
Duck through the low doorway into the cabin and settle onto the cushions.

60
00:07:59,750 --> 00:08:05,462
Inside, it is snug and dim, and smells faintly of wool and wood and the sea.

61
00:08:05,462 --> 00:08:13,656
Through the little window, you can see the lamps of the landing, and the dark water moving softly beside you.

62
00:08:15,156 --> 00:08:20,454
Your gondolier stands at the stern, behind you, high above the water.

63
00:08:20,454 --> 00:08:35,043
He rows standing up, facing forward, with one long oar resting in a carved wooden oarlock, a curved and twisted piece of walnut that lets him push, and turn, and slow, all with the same oar.

64
00:08:35,043 --> 00:08:40,265
He leans into the first stroke, and the landing slides quietly away.

65
00:08:41,765 --> 00:08:52,091
Boats like this one are built by hand in small boatyards called squeri, tucked beside the canals, where the ground slopes gently down to the water.

66
00:08:52,091 --> 00:09:05,928
There, beneath wide wooden roofs, the builders shape each gondola from several different kinds of timber, oak and larch, elm and walnut, cherry and lime, each one chosen for its own particular job.

67
00:09:05,928 --> 00:09:12,250
A good gondola takes many weeks to make, and the builders know every curve of it by heart.

68
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:15,648
There is almost no sound.

69
00:09:15,648 --> 00:09:22,252
Just the soft dip of the oar, a little swirl of water, a pause, and then the dip again.

70
00:09:22,252 --> 00:09:37,131
The gondola glides forward between the tall walls of the houses, and the lamplight from the windows passes slowly over the black cloth of your little cabin, one window, then another, then another.

71
00:09:38,632 --> 00:09:50,387
At a corner where two canals meet, the gondolier calls out a single word, long and low, so that any boat coming the other way will hear him before it sees him.

72
00:09:50,387 --> 00:09:54,157
Somewhere beyond the corner, another voice answers.

73
00:09:54,157 --> 00:10:01,181
The two boats pass each other with an arm's length to spare, and then the water is quiet again.

74
00:10:02,681 --> 00:10:14,995
Out on the wider water, you pass one of the ferry stations, a little wooden landing where a few plain boats wait to carry people from one bank to the other for a small coin.

75
00:10:14,995 --> 00:10:25,957
A woman with a basket stands in one of them, perfectly balanced, as it crosses the canal, and a dog sits patiently at her feet, looking out at the lights.

76
00:10:25,957 --> 00:10:32,078
In a few moments, they are on the far side, and the ferryman turns back to wait again.

77
00:10:41,578 --> 00:10:48,545
The gondola bumps gently against a step, and you climb out onto the stones of a small square.

78
00:10:48,545 --> 00:10:52,366
In Venice, the open squares are not called piazzas.

79
00:10:52,366 --> 00:10:58,358
There is only one piazza, the great square in front of the church of Saint Mark.

80
00:10:58,358 --> 00:11:10,869
Every other square, large or small, is called a campo, a field, because long ago many of them really were grassy fields, with gardens and orchards and grazing animals.

81
00:11:12,369 --> 00:11:22,548
This campo is paved now, with brick laid in a herringbone pattern, and it is ringed by houses with shutters and small balconies.

82
00:11:22,548 --> 00:11:28,353
On one side stands a parish church, with a tall bell tower of rosy brick.

83
00:11:28,353 --> 00:11:38,929
On the other side, an old woman is sitting on a doorstep, enjoying the last of the mild evening air, with a shawl over her shoulders.

84
00:11:40,429 --> 00:11:44,392
In the middle of the square, there is a wellhead.

85
00:11:44,392 --> 00:11:53,936
It is a heavy round drum of carved stone, about waist high, decorated with leaves and shields and a worn coat of arms.

86
00:11:53,936 --> 00:12:00,811
Its rim has been rubbed smooth by generations of ropes and buckets and resting hands.

87
00:12:00,811 --> 00:12:04,450
An iron lid covers the opening for the night.

88
00:12:05,950 --> 00:12:14,342
It is strange, when you think about it, a city surrounded on every side by water, and yet thirsty for it.

89
00:12:14,342 --> 00:12:15,861
The lagoon is salt.

90
00:12:15,861 --> 00:12:17,939
So Venice drinks the rain.

91
00:12:17,939 --> 00:12:28,649
Under almost every campo there is a hidden cistern, a great pit lined with thick clay to keep the sea out, and filled with clean sand.

92
00:12:30,149 --> 00:12:38,415
Look down at the paving, and you will notice that the square slopes very gently towards a few small stone grates.

93
00:12:38,415 --> 00:12:48,071
When it rains, the water runs over the bricks, slips through those grates, and soaks slowly down through the sand, which filters it.

94
00:12:48,071 --> 00:12:56,922
At the centre, a shaft of brick gathers the clean water, and the wellhead sits on top of that shaft, like a lid on a jar.

95
00:12:58,422 --> 00:13:06,215
In many squares, the iron lid on the well is locked at night, and someone from the parish keeps the key.

96
00:13:06,215 --> 00:13:10,037
Water is precious here, and it is shared carefully.

97
00:13:10,037 --> 00:13:22,401
In the morning, at the proper hour, the lid will be opened, and the buckets will go down, one after another, on their long ropes, and come back up dripping and cold.

98
00:13:23,901 --> 00:13:37,451
In dry summers, when the cisterns run low, boatmen bring fresh water across the lagoon from a river on the mainland, in broad, low barges, and pour it carefully into the wells.

99
00:13:37,451 --> 00:13:39,915
But tonight the cistern is full.

100
00:13:39,915 --> 00:13:49,693
It rained two days ago, and the sand beneath your feet is still holding the cool, sweet water, slowly, patiently, out of sight.

101
00:13:51,193 --> 00:13:54,740
In the morning, this square will be full of noise.

102
00:13:54,740 --> 00:14:03,040
Women with copper buckets on a yoke across their shoulders, talking and laughing as they wait their turn at the well.

103
00:14:03,040 --> 00:14:05,735
Children chasing each other around it.

104
00:14:05,735 --> 00:14:07,438
A man selling chestnuts.

105
00:14:07,438 --> 00:14:20,420
But for now, there is only the old woman on her doorstep, and a cat walking slowly along the rim of the well, and above it all, the bell tower, dark against a sky full of quiet stars.

106
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:32,279
Up on the rooftops around the campo, you can see small wooden platforms built on top of the houses, like little decks perched among the chimneys.

107
00:14:32,279 --> 00:14:33,922
They are called altane.

108
00:14:33,922 --> 00:14:43,067
In the daytime, people climb up to them to hang their washing, or to sit in the sun and the breeze, high above the narrow lanes.

109
00:14:43,067 --> 00:14:50,784
Tonight, a few white sheets are still pegged out on one of them, stirring very slowly in the mild night air.

110
00:15:00,284 --> 00:15:06,781
Now we walk a little way through the lanes, to the busiest place in all of Venice.

111
00:15:06,781 --> 00:15:18,588
Here, the Grand Canal, the great curving waterway that winds through the middle of the city like a backwards letter S, is crossed by a single bridge.

112
00:15:18,588 --> 00:15:20,252
The bridge at Rialto.

113
00:15:21,752 --> 00:15:24,272
Tonight's bridge is made of wood.

114
00:15:24,272 --> 00:15:29,085
There have been several wooden bridges here over the centuries.

115
00:15:29,085 --> 00:15:32,522
One was damaged during an uprising, long ago.

116
00:15:32,522 --> 00:15:42,529
Another collapsed in fourteen forty four, under the weight of a crowd that had gathered on it to watch a grand procession of boats.

117
00:15:42,529 --> 00:15:46,349
Each time, the Venetians patiently built it again.

118
00:15:47,849 --> 00:15:57,276
This one is a long, heavy timber bridge, with two rows of little shops running along its sides, their shutters closed for the night.

119
00:15:57,276 --> 00:16:07,560
In the very middle, the bridge rises higher, and the central section was made so that it could be raised, to let tall-masted ships pass through.

120
00:16:07,560 --> 00:16:19,486
You can find this very bridge, painted in loving detail, in pictures by the Venetian painter Carpaccio, with its shops and its crowds, and gondolas sliding beneath it.

121
00:16:20,986 --> 00:16:28,298
For some years now, people in the city have been talking about replacing it with a bridge of stone.

122
00:16:28,298 --> 00:16:31,031
Famous architects have drawn designs.

123
00:16:31,031 --> 00:16:34,354
Councils have met, and argued, and met again.

124
00:16:34,354 --> 00:16:40,779
It will be some years yet before the white stone bridge that we know today is finished.

125
00:16:40,779 --> 00:16:47,205
For now, the old wooden one creaks softly underfoot, and smells of tar and damp timber.

126
00:16:48,705 --> 00:16:53,500
Around the foot of the bridge lies the great market of Rialto.

127
00:16:53,500 --> 00:16:56,517
During the day, it is a roar of voices.

128
00:16:56,517 --> 00:16:59,301
Fishmongers calling out their catch.

129
00:16:59,301 --> 00:17:03,865
Greengrocers stacking melons and figs and baskets of herbs.

130
00:17:03,865 --> 00:17:09,898
Bankers sitting at their tables beneath the arcades, writing in thick ledgers.

131
00:17:09,898 --> 00:17:17,014
Merchants from every corner of the Mediterranean, arguing over the price of pepper and silk.

132
00:17:18,514 --> 00:17:29,465
Near the bridge, there is a small church, so old that the story goes it was founded on the very day the city itself began, more than a thousand years earlier.

133
00:17:29,465 --> 00:17:41,733
In the little square in front of it stands a stone platform with a short flight of steps, held up by a crouching stone figure, a small, bent man carrying the weight on his back.

134
00:17:41,733 --> 00:17:48,872
From the top of those steps, the Republic's officials read out new laws and announcements to the crowd.

135
00:17:50,372 --> 00:17:53,988
But it is evening now, and the market is closing.

136
00:17:53,988 --> 00:18:01,589
The fish stalls have been washed down with buckets of lagoon water, and the stones are wet and shining.

137
00:18:01,589 --> 00:18:04,541
A man sweeps cabbage leaves into a heap.

138
00:18:04,541 --> 00:18:10,741
Two boys are carrying a long pole between them, with empty baskets swinging from it.

139
00:18:10,741 --> 00:18:17,678
The last barges are being poled away, low in the water, towards the islands where they belong.

140
00:18:19,178 --> 00:18:29,608
Just along the canal stands a great warehouse of the German merchants, with a courtyard in the middle and painted walls facing the water.

141
00:18:29,608 --> 00:18:37,526
On those walls, two young painters, Giorgione and Titian, once painted enormous figures, many years ago.

142
00:18:37,526 --> 00:18:46,890
In the fading light you can only just make out the colours, soft reds and golds, already a little weathered by the sea air.

143
00:18:48,390 --> 00:18:54,384
Stand for a moment at the top of the bridge, and look along the Grand Canal.

144
00:18:54,384 --> 00:19:02,428
Palaces line both banks, their stone balconies and pointed windows reflected upside down in the water.

145
00:19:02,428 --> 00:19:05,030
Lanterns hang by the water gates.

146
00:19:05,030 --> 00:19:10,551
Gondolas glide slowly up and down, each with a small light at the bow.

147
00:19:10,551 --> 00:19:17,254
And the whole long curve of the canal glimmers, softly, all the way to the next bend.

148
00:19:18,754 --> 00:19:24,035
Beneath your feet, a gondola passes under the bridge, slow and silent.

149
00:19:24,035 --> 00:19:44,632
For a moment, you can see the small lantern at its bow shining up on the wet timbers of the arch, and then it slides out on the other side, and carries on down the canal, growing smaller and smaller, until it is just one more little light among all the others on the water.

150
00:19:54,132 --> 00:19:59,471
Away from the Grand Canal, the lanes of Venice are narrow and winding.

151
00:19:59,471 --> 00:20:08,166
Some of them are so narrow that you could touch the walls on both sides at once, just by stretching out your arms.

152
00:20:08,166 --> 00:20:18,387
They twist, and turn, and cross little bridges, and sometimes they end suddenly at the edge of a canal, with nothing but water beyond.

153
00:20:19,888 --> 00:20:25,746
After dark, these lanes would be very black indeed, if not for small lights.

154
00:20:25,746 --> 00:20:36,769
Centuries ago, the story goes, a Doge ordered little oil lamps to be hung on the walls of the darkest corners, to make the city safer at night.

155
00:20:36,769 --> 00:20:46,405
They were tiny things, hardly more than a cup of oil and a floating wick, but each one made a small, warm circle in the dark.

156
00:20:47,905 --> 00:20:52,401
Many of the lights in the lanes are set before small shrines.

157
00:20:52,401 --> 00:21:04,709
Here and there, on a corner, there is a little painted image of the Madonna, framed in carved stone or wood, sometimes with a small roof above it to keep off the rain.

158
00:21:04,709 --> 00:21:10,752
In front of it, a lamp burns, and sometimes a few flowers have been left in a jar.

159
00:21:10,752 --> 00:21:17,238
Neighbours take turns to keep the lamp filled with oil, so that it burns all night long.

160
00:21:18,738 --> 00:21:23,010
There is a gentle legend in Venice about one such light.

161
00:21:23,010 --> 00:21:34,607
The story goes that, many years ago, a young baker was wrongly accused of a crime and punished for it, and only afterwards was his innocence discovered.

162
00:21:34,607 --> 00:21:48,797
Ever since, it is said, a lamp has been kept burning high on the side of the great church of Saint Mark, in his memory, and as a reminder to judges to think carefully before they decide.

163
00:21:50,297 --> 00:21:55,531
People who go out at night are expected to carry a light of their own.

164
00:21:55,531 --> 00:22:04,655
Wealthy households send a servant walking ahead with a lantern, its glass panels throwing soft yellow shapes on the walls.

165
00:22:04,655 --> 00:22:11,534
Ordinary folk carry a small lamp, or a candle in a little box, cupped carefully in one hand.

166
00:22:11,534 --> 00:22:18,414
All through the city, small lights are moving, slowly, along the lanes and over the bridges.

167
00:22:19,914 --> 00:22:22,269
Follow one of them for a while.

168
00:22:22,269 --> 00:22:26,446
A man in a long dark cloak, walking home from his work.

169
00:22:26,446 --> 00:22:34,574
His lantern swings gently, and his shadow stretches out along the wall, then shrinks, then stretches again.

170
00:22:34,574 --> 00:22:45,663
He crosses a little bridge, nods to a neighbour on a balcony above, and turns down a passage so narrow that his lantern almost brushes the bricks.

171
00:22:47,163 --> 00:22:59,466
Here and there, a lane ducks right underneath a house, through a low, dark passage with wooden beams overhead, where the upper floors of the building simply carry on above you.

172
00:22:59,466 --> 00:23:03,311
The Venetians call these covered passages sotoporteghi.

173
00:23:03,311 --> 00:23:16,731
Walking through one at night is like passing through a short tunnel, with the sound of your footsteps close around you, and then out again, into the open air, beside the soft gleam of a canal.

174
00:23:18,231 --> 00:23:21,438
At the end of the passage, a door opens.

175
00:23:21,438 --> 00:23:24,485
Warm light spills out onto the stones.

176
00:23:24,485 --> 00:23:30,979
You hear the clink of a pot, a child's sleepy voice, and someone laughing softly.

177
00:23:30,979 --> 00:23:40,759
Then the door closes, and the passage is quiet again, lit only by the steady little flame before the shrine on the corner.

178
00:23:50,259 --> 00:24:01,093
At the eastern end of the city, behind high brick walls with battlements along the top, lies the Arsenal, the great shipyard of the Republic.

179
00:24:01,093 --> 00:24:06,241
During the day, it is the busiest place in Venice after the market.

180
00:24:06,241 --> 00:24:18,612
Thousands of workers, carpenters, caulkers, rope makers, sail makers, smiths, all labouring together to build the ships that carry Venetian trade across the sea.

181
00:24:20,112 --> 00:24:26,667
Every working morning, a great bell in the tower of Saint Mark calls them to the yard.

182
00:24:26,667 --> 00:24:36,959
It is the deepest bell of all, and the Venetians gave it a name of its own, the Marangona, which comes from their word for a carpenter.

183
00:24:36,959 --> 00:24:42,448
It rings again in the evening, telling them that the day's work is done.

184
00:24:43,948 --> 00:24:53,831
Inside the walls, there are long rows of covered sheds, with tall roofs on wooden pillars, standing side by side along the water.

185
00:24:53,831 --> 00:25:10,764
The ships were built in stages, moving from one place to the next, so that a hull might arrive at one shed and leave it a little more complete, while stores of oars, masts, and fittings waited, ready, in great warehouses.

186
00:25:12,264 --> 00:25:21,287
Along one side of the yard stands a building that seems to go on for ever, a very long, narrow hall, where the rope makers work.

187
00:25:21,287 --> 00:25:35,246
To make the great ropes that a ship needs, they walk slowly backwards down the length of the hall, twisting strands of hemp as they go, again and again, until each rope is long and thick and strong.

188
00:25:35,246 --> 00:25:40,040
Tonight, the long hall is empty, and smells faintly of hemp and tar.

189
00:25:41,540 --> 00:25:48,805
The story goes that when a young king of France came to visit Venice, he was taken to see the Arsenal.

190
00:25:48,805 --> 00:25:55,074
Before he sat down to a grand banquet, he watched the workers begin assembling a galley.

191
00:25:55,074 --> 00:26:00,987
By the time the meal was finished, the ship was complete, and ready to be launched.

192
00:26:00,987 --> 00:26:05,688
The tale may have grown a little in the telling, as good tales do.

193
00:26:05,688 --> 00:26:10,105
But it shows how proud the Venetians were of their great yard.

194
00:26:11,605 --> 00:26:23,079
Long ago, the poet Dante saw this place, and remembered the workers boiling sticky pitch in great cauldrons, to seal the seams of the ships.

195
00:26:23,079 --> 00:26:31,274
In the daytime, the air still smells of it, warm and dark and resinous, drifting out over the water.

196
00:26:32,774 --> 00:26:39,651
The workers of the Arsenal were proud of their craft, and the Republic honoured them for it.

197
00:26:39,651 --> 00:26:53,182
On great feast days, it was the men of the Arsenal who rowed the Doge's magnificent ceremonial barge, all red and gold, out across the lagoon, with banners flying and music playing.

198
00:26:53,182 --> 00:27:00,358
But tonight they are at home, in the little streets near the yard, asleep beside their families.

199
00:27:01,858 --> 00:27:04,075
Tonight, all of that is still.

200
00:27:04,075 --> 00:27:05,627
The gates are closed.

201
00:27:05,627 --> 00:27:11,095
The sheds stand silent in long rows, their tall roofs pale under the moon.

202
00:27:11,095 --> 00:27:22,401
A watchman walks slowly along the top of the wall, carrying a lantern, and then stops for a while, leaning on the parapet, looking out at the calm water.

203
00:27:22,401 --> 00:27:28,461
Somewhere inside, a half-built hull rests on its timbers, waiting for the morning.

204
00:27:29,961 --> 00:27:43,832
Across the water from the Arsenal, on a small island of its own, a new church is slowly rising, designed by the architect Palladio, all calm white columns and rounded arches.

205
00:27:43,832 --> 00:27:51,565
Tonight, it is wrapped in scaffolding, and the moonlight falls softly on the half-finished stone.

206
00:27:51,565 --> 00:27:54,435
It will take many years to complete.

207
00:27:54,435 --> 00:27:55,710
But it has time.

208
00:27:55,710 --> 00:27:58,740
It is in no hurry, and neither are we.

209
00:28:08,240 --> 00:28:24,552
The gondola carries you north now, through a long, quiet canal lined with the backs of houses, past small gardens behind high walls, where fig trees lean over the brickwork and a few late roses are still blooming.

210
00:28:24,552 --> 00:28:32,517
The gondolier's oar dips, and lifts, and dips again, and the water whispers along the sides of the boat.

211
00:28:34,017 --> 00:28:42,984
The houses open out, and suddenly you are at the edge of the lagoon again, looking north across the dark water.

212
00:28:42,984 --> 00:28:50,255
And there, a little way off, on a cluster of low islands, you can see a faint orange glow.

213
00:28:50,255 --> 00:28:54,941
It flickers, very softly, on the undersides of the clouds.

214
00:28:54,941 --> 00:28:58,657
That is Murano, the island of the glassmakers.

215
00:29:00,157 --> 00:29:10,002
On the way, you pass a small island with a walled monastery and a pale stone church, its front gleaming softly in the moonlight.

216
00:29:10,002 --> 00:29:26,461
The water around it is perfectly smooth, and the reflections of its walls and of a few tall trees stand upside down beneath them, so that the whole little island seems to have a twin, reaching down into the lagoon.

217
00:29:27,961 --> 00:29:37,458
Long ago, in twelve ninety one, the Republic ordered almost all the glass furnaces in Venice to be moved out to Murano.

218
00:29:37,458 --> 00:29:49,348
A city built so tightly, with so much timber in its roofs and floors and foundations, was terrified of fire, and glass furnaces burn very hot indeed.

219
00:29:49,348 --> 00:29:56,292
So the glassmakers moved across the water, and Murano became a little world of its own.

220
00:29:57,792 --> 00:30:04,972
Inside the workshops, the furnaces are domes of brick, glowing at their mouths like small suns.

221
00:30:04,972 --> 00:30:11,775
The glassmakers sit on wooden benches beside them, turning long iron pipes in their hands.

222
00:30:11,775 --> 00:30:28,554
On the end of each pipe, a glowing blob of molten glass slowly swells, and stretches, and takes shape, a goblet, a bowl, a delicate little vase, as the maker breathes into the pipe and rolls it along the arms of his bench.

223
00:30:30,054 --> 00:30:32,896
Venetian glass was famous everywhere.

224
00:30:32,896 --> 00:30:44,802
In the previous century, the glassmakers here had learned to make a glass so clear and colourless that it was called cristallo, as if it were rock crystal.

225
00:30:44,802 --> 00:30:59,857
Fine tables in every great city of Europe wanted Venetian glasses, Venetian mirrors, and Venetian beads, and so the master glassmakers of Murano were treasured, and protected, and closely watched.

226
00:31:01,357 --> 00:31:04,585
Murano is not only furnaces and workshops.

227
00:31:04,585 --> 00:31:22,727
It is also a quiet place of gardens and orchards, of small canals and old churches, and some of the noble families of Venice keep summer houses there, with shady loggias and long garden walks, where they come to escape the crowded city.

228
00:31:22,727 --> 00:31:29,261
On an autumn night, the gardens are still, and smell of damp earth and fallen leaves.

229
00:31:30,761 --> 00:31:35,823
From here, across the water, you cannot hear the workshops at all.

230
00:31:35,823 --> 00:31:41,728
You can only see the glow, warm and steady, a soft amber smudge on the night.

231
00:31:41,728 --> 00:31:54,536
Imagine, in one of those workshops, a young apprentice, his work finished, sitting by the cooling furnace, watching the last of the coals turn from orange to deep red.

232
00:31:54,536 --> 00:31:55,993
His eyes are heavy.

233
00:31:55,993 --> 00:31:58,217
His head is beginning to nod.

234
00:31:59,717 --> 00:32:11,342
The apprentice wraps himself in his cloak and lies down on a pallet of straw in the corner, where the brick of the furnace wall still gives off a gentle warmth.

235
00:32:11,342 --> 00:32:14,902
Outside, the canal laps softly against the steps.

236
00:32:14,902 --> 00:32:25,510
He listens to it for a little while, and to the faint creak of a boat at its mooring, and to the slow ticking of the cooling glass on the shelves.

237
00:32:25,510 --> 00:32:27,109
And then he is asleep.

238
00:32:28,609 --> 00:32:34,214
The gondolier rests on his oar for a while, and lets the boat drift.

239
00:32:34,214 --> 00:32:37,430
Small waves lap against the black hull.

240
00:32:37,430 --> 00:32:46,250
The glow from Murano trembles on the water in a long, broken line, reaching almost all the way to the boat.

241
00:32:46,250 --> 00:32:48,641
And everything is very quiet.

242
00:32:58,141 --> 00:33:02,228
Slowly, the gondola turns back towards the city.

243
00:33:02,228 --> 00:33:04,698
The gondolier is in no hurry.

244
00:33:04,698 --> 00:33:06,060
Neither are you.

245
00:33:06,060 --> 00:33:09,551
The lamps along the canals are fewer now.

246
00:33:09,551 --> 00:33:18,322
Many of the windows have gone dark, one by one, as families pinch out their candles and climb into bed.

247
00:33:19,822 --> 00:33:26,690
Far off, from the tower of Saint Mark, a bell rings out over the water, slow and deep.

248
00:33:26,690 --> 00:33:27,568
It is late.

249
00:33:27,568 --> 00:33:43,140
In the old days of the Republic, a bell like this told the city that the night had truly begun, that it was time for the taverns to close and for the streets to empty and for everyone to go home.

250
00:33:44,640 --> 00:33:47,257
Imagine the whole city settling.

251
00:33:47,257 --> 00:33:54,127
In a palace on the Grand Canal, a merchant closes his ledger and blows out his lamp.

252
00:33:54,127 --> 00:34:02,061
In a small house beside a campo, a mother tucks a woollen blanket around three sleeping children.

253
00:34:02,061 --> 00:34:10,240
In the Arsenal, the watchman finishes his round and sits down by a small brazier, warming his hands.

254
00:34:11,740 --> 00:34:23,687
In a monastery on one of the little islands, a single monk is still awake, reading by a candle, his finger moving slowly along the lines of a heavy book.

255
00:34:23,687 --> 00:34:28,137
After a while, his eyes close, and open, and close again.

256
00:34:28,137 --> 00:34:36,102
He marks his page with a ribbon, pinches out the flame, and the island goes dark, like all the others.

257
00:34:37,602 --> 00:34:44,679
Out on the lagoon, the mist is thickening a little, soft and white, curling low over the water.

258
00:34:44,679 --> 00:34:48,329
The mooring posts stand in it like quiet figures.

259
00:34:48,329 --> 00:34:52,202
The heron has gone to sleep, somewhere in the reeds.

260
00:34:52,202 --> 00:35:04,717
Only the tide moves now, slowly, patiently, as it has moved here twice a day for thousands of years, washing in and washing out, around the stones of the sleeping city.

261
00:35:06,217 --> 00:35:08,577
Lean back against the cushions.

262
00:35:08,577 --> 00:35:12,307
Let the gentle sway of the boat move through you.

263
00:35:12,307 --> 00:35:15,352
The oar dips, and rises, and dips again.

264
00:35:15,352 --> 00:35:22,432
The lamplight slides slowly past the little window, warm, and then gone, and then warm again.

265
00:35:22,432 --> 00:35:25,781
You do not need to watch where we are going.

266
00:35:25,781 --> 00:35:27,913
The gondolier knows the way.

267
00:35:29,413 --> 00:35:32,883
Breathe slowly with the rhythm of the oar.

268
00:35:32,883 --> 00:35:36,022
In, as the blade lifts from the water.

269
00:35:36,022 --> 00:35:38,170
Out, as it slides back in.

270
00:35:38,170 --> 00:35:39,162
In, and out.

271
00:35:39,162 --> 00:35:51,472
The boat moves forward a little with each breath, and the stone walls of the city drift past, soft and grey and silent, and the water carries you on.

272
00:35:52,972 --> 00:36:04,520
Soon the boat will slip back into the canals, and glide beneath the little bridges, and come to rest at a quiet landing beside a door with a lamp above it.

273
00:36:04,520 --> 00:36:07,276
But you do not need to wait for that.

274
00:36:07,276 --> 00:36:12,492
You can let the gondola carry you the rest of the way while you sleep.

275
00:36:13,992 --> 00:36:19,535
Thank you for floating through Venice with The Quiet Archive tonight.

276
00:36:19,535 --> 00:36:24,998
Let the water hold you, and let the night be long and soft and calm.

277
00:36:24,998 --> 00:36:25,881
Sleep well.

278
00:36:25,881 --> 00:36:26,685
Goodnight.
